<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The menu-bar bundle (D27). A SECOND bundle, deliberately: the daemon's own Info.plist next door
  exists solely as the TCC anchor of D11, and everything about it is arranged so that it is not an
  application in any other sense. This one is the application, and it is a different binary with a
  different identity so that the daemon's shape — and the microphone grant recorded against it —
  does not move because dicta grew a menu.

  Note what is ABSENT and must stay absent:

    * NSMicrophoneUsageDescription. This bundle never opens the device, so it must never be in a
      position to ask; a second binary opening the microphone would fracture the grant (D11,
      invariant 8). `Scripts/linkage.sh` checks the built binary for the same claim.
    * Any document type, URL scheme or service. The menu is a lamp and a receipt drawer, not an app
      other software can hand work to.

  CFBundleIdentifier is `dev.personal.dicta.menu` — a child of the daemon's identifier by spelling
  and by nothing else. It must NOT be `dev.personal.dicta`: two bundles sharing an identifier is how
  a TCC grant gets attributed to whichever one macOS saw last, which is the failure D11 exists to
  prevent, arriving through a new door.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>DictaMenu</string>
    <!-- What a human sees. The bundle directory is `DictaMenu.app` with no space, because every
         script that handles it runs under macOS's stock bash 3.2 and a space in a path is a class
         of bug this project has already paid for elsewhere. -->
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Dicta Menu</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>DictaMenu</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>dev.personal.dicta.menu</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.1.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <!-- No dock icon and no ⌘-tab entry. A menu-bar item is the whole surface (D27), and a dock
         tile would put a dictation one stray click away from stealing focus from the terminal it is
         about to type into — the same reason the daemon carries this key. It also keeps D22 honest:
         the less this bundle can be activated by accident, the less often agterm stops being
         frontmost for reasons the user did not intend. -->
    <key>LSUIElement</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>14.0</string>
</dict>
</plist>
